{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"reasoning","text":"Marker genes with strict criteria are few in number, and the bulk of expression (3664 out of 3693 UMIs) is shared (\"non-block\") expression. So this simulation is composed of shared/housekeeping expression + a small number of cell-type-specific marker genes. This explains why cosine nearest-neighbor voting is dominated by shared genes: all cells share the same program, and the differences lie in the sparse marker genes.\n\nGiven this structure, deconvolution based on the full transcriptome is dominated by the shared component. A better approach for this data: leverage the cell-type-specific genes. However, the strict criterion (mean > 1, others < 0.5) yields only 1–4 genes per cell type. Let's loosen the criterion: define cell-type-differential genes with a more relaxed threshold and see how marker expression is distributed within the spot.\n\nAlternative: fit NNLS against marker genes only. Or better yet: use a proper probabilistic approach. But a simple, reasonable main method is: NNLS against cell-type mean profiles restricted to genes that carry cell-type information (differential genes), or full-profile NNLS (already run). Full-profile NNLS gave Endothelial 0.354, B_Cell 0.317, Macrophage 0.317, T_Cell 0.012 — roughly equal thirds, which strongly suggests that the true composition is a 3-way mixture. But is it really? The neighbor voting was all Endothelial. Because of the shared expression, the cosine similarities among mixtures of similar profiles are... no, wait. If the spot is a mixture of Endothelial + B_Cell + Macrophage, the spot profile contains marker genes from all three cell types, and a pure Endothelial cell has its own markers but not the others. With cosine similarity on the full profile, the shared component dominates, so the nearest cell is Endothelial anyway (the shared program makes all cells similar to each other; cos ~0.65).\n\nLet's do a definitive check: reconstruc